From 635054201592d5f881e4cbd216ba36f78fc1557e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Petr=20=C5=A0tetiar?= <petr.stetiar@prplfoundation.org>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 23:15:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] AP MLD: guard hostapd_bss_link_deinit() against a never-added
 link

hostapd_setup_bss() (src/ap/hostapd.c:1425) adds a non-first MLD link
in two steps: hostapd_drv_link_add() first asks the driver/kernel to
create the link, and only if that call succeeds does it call
hostapd_mld_add_link() (:1530-1538), which is what actually puts
hapd->link on the mld->links dl_list and bumps mld->num_links. If
hostapd_drv_link_add() fails, hostapd_setup_bss() returns -1 straight
away and hapd->link stays exactly as os_zalloc() left it: a
zero-filled dl_list node with next == prev == NULL, never linked into
any list.

The interface-teardown path does not know about this distinction.
hostapd_bss_link_deinit() (:647-681) unconditionally calls
hostapd_mld_remove_link() once its three existing guards pass
(non-empty mld->links, hapd->started or bss[0], and
bss[0]==hapd => drv_priv set) -- none of which detect "this bss was
never linked". hostapd_mld_remove_link() then runs
dl_list_del(&hapd->link) (:5029) on the zeroed node: dl_list_del()
dereferences item->prev to fix up its ->next pointer, and with
prev == NULL that is a NULL-plus-offsetof(next) write and a
segfault.

This is reachable whenever a later BSS of an MLD interface fails
hostapd_drv_link_add() during hostapd_add_iface()'s per-BSS
setup_interface2() loop (hostapd.c:2672-2683): the loop's own cleanup
only calls hostapd_bss_deinit_no_free() + hostapd_free_hapd_data(),
"goto fail" bubbles up through hostapd_setup_interface_complete_sync()
and hostapd_add_iface() (:3878-3884), and the bottom of
hostapd_interface_deinit() (:3985-4005) calls
hostapd_bss_link_deinit() on every bss[], including the one whose
link_add never landed. Observed in practice on a warm MLD-link re-add
after an earlier hostapd_setup_interface_complete_sync() failure on
the same radio left a driver-level link that a retry then collides
with (hostapd_drv_link_add() -EALREADY): "MLD: Failed to add link %d
in MLD %s" immediately followed by a segfault in dl_list_del(), fault
address 8 (write), matching a NULL item->prev.

Not fixed upstream: hostap.git HEAD (88142b806fbb, 2025-08-26) has a
byte-identical hostapd_bss_link_deinit() / hostapd_mld_remove_link().
The existing !mld->num_links guard (69deac87fbf6, 2024-04-30) covers
only the case where no link of this MLD was ever added; it does not
cover a specific bss whose own link add failed while sibling links
did succeed.

Fix: hapd->link.next stays NULL for a node that was never added to
mld->links (dl_list_del() also NULLs both pointers again after a real
removal, so the same check is double-delete safe). Return early from
hostapd_bss_link_deinit() before hostapd_mld_remove_link() when it is
NULL, instead of dereferencing a never-linked or already-removed
node.

The companion leak -- hostapd_drv_link_add() can itself succeed and
then a later step inside hostapd_setup_bss() can still fail and
return -1 without a matching hostapd_if_link_remove(), leaking the
driver-level link that the next add then collides with -- is left
alone here: hostapd_setup_bss() has dozens of unrelated "return -1;"
sites after the link_add call, threading a driver-level unwind
through all of them is not a small change, and this segfault guard
already makes the crash chain go away regardless.

Generic: not specific to this testbed. Any upstream-mac80211 hostapd
build that warm-adds/removes MLD links through the control interface
can hit this NULL dereference once a link add fails.
---
 src/ap/hostapd.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/ap/hostapd.c b/src/ap/hostapd.c
index f6dfa9679045..098a224010c1 100644
--- a/src/ap/hostapd.c
+++ b/src/ap/hostapd.c
@@ -677,6 +677,18 @@ static void hostapd_bss_link_deinit(struct hostapd_data *hapd)
 	if (hapd->iface->bss[0] == hapd && !hapd->drv_priv)
 		return;
 
+	/* If hostapd_drv_link_add() in hostapd_setup_bss() never succeeded
+	 * (e.g. -EALREADY on a leaked kernel link left by an earlier failed
+	 * add), hapd->link was never added to mld->links: it is still the
+	 * zeroed dl_list node from hapd's allocation, with next == prev ==
+	 * NULL. Calling hostapd_mld_remove_link() -> dl_list_del() on it
+	 * dereferences that NULL prev pointer and crashes. dl_list_del()
+	 * also leaves both pointers NULL after a real removal, so this
+	 * check is double-delete safe too.
+	 */
+	if (!hapd->link.next)
+		return;
+
 	hostapd_mld_remove_link(hapd);
 #endif /* CONFIG_IEEE80211BE */
 }
